# Contracts and source packages

Trellis contracts are native `.trellis` source packages. A package manifest
declares identity/version, explicitly listed source files, dependencies, and
generated Rust or TypeScript destinations. `trellis.lock` freezes exact package
versions and semantic/distribution digests.

## APIs and participants

APIs define RPCs, durable Operations, Events, Live observations, API-scoped errors, and
capabilities. Participants are services, devices, apps, or agents. A device may
contain one named app or agent companion as its lexical child. The child is
activated only through its parent device and has a separate user-owned grant,
login session, authorization context, resources, and connection; parent and
child authority never merge.

Companion consent is computed from the installed child revision and current
user authority. Approval repeats the decision digest, installed revision, and
expected grant revision and may select only the eligible capabilities and
resources in that consent. Stale or mismatched approval is rejected.

## Identity and evidence

Capabilities explain groups of actions for approval but do not directly grant
authority. Trellis derives exact selected runtime grants from approved scopes,
public membership, current ceilings, and usable resource bindings. Concrete
resource commitments are approvals; desired capacities are only hints.

## Generated packages

The compiler builds a native semantic graph and package digest. Published OCI
bundles contain source plus frozen resolution metadata. Generated codecs and
descriptors are SDK projections, not canonical JSON artifacts or a
contract-as-code builder surface.

Compatibility is selected-surface and direction-aware. Package versions are
metadata, not authorization. Installed semantic revisions remain immutable for
context explanation and retained event-history verification.
